(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xix-d"] = {"qs": [["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A strategic buyer may pay more than a financial buyer because it:", ["Is required by SEBI to pay a premium", "Avoids due diligence", "Can realise synergies with its existing business", "Does not need to finance the deal"], 2, "Synergies in costs or revenues allow strategic acquirers to justify higher prices."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "Why do investors prefer a whole-of-fund waterfall to a deal-by-deal waterfall?", ["Carry is paid only after all contributed capital is returned", "The manager receives carry earlier, which improves alignment with investors", "It removes hurdle rates", "It increases management fees"], 0, "Whole-of-fund waterfalls defer carry until investors recover capital and the hurdle, reducing clawback risk."], ["AIF Structuring", "A trust deed for an AIF mainly sets out:", ["The detailed terms of each portfolio company investment the fund will make", "The salary of the manager's employees", "The trust's objectives and powers of the trustee", "Daily NAV of the fund"], 2, "The trust deed creates the trust and defines its objects, the trustee's powers and the beneficiaries."], ["Investment Strategies", "A growth equity fund typically takes:", ["Minority stakes in profitable or scaling companies", "Majority control in every deal, using heavy acquisition debt", "Only debt instruments", "Stakes only in listed blue chips"], 0, "Growth equity provides expansion capital through minority stakes with lower leverage than buyouts."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund's DPI is low but TVPI is high in year eight. This means:", ["Most of the value is still unrealised", "Most value has already been returned", "The fund has negative returns", "Fees have been waived"], 0, "A high TVPI with low DPI shows value sits in NAV rather than cash returned, so realisation risk remains."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A public market equivalent analysis shows a fund under-performed the Nifty on a cash-flow-matched basis. This means:", ["The fund outperformed the index after adjusting for the timing of cash flows", "The fund's IRR was negative", "The index cannot be compared", "Investing the same flows in the index would have earned more"], 3, "PME compares the fund with a hypothetical index investment using the same cash flows; underperformance means the index route would have done better."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Who is responsible for ensuring that the AIF's operations comply with SEBI regulations on a daily basis?", ["The manager, through its compliance officer", "The investee companies", "The investors", "The statutory auditor of the investee companies, through its annual audit"], 0, "The manager is responsible for compliance and must appoint a compliance officer to monitor it."], ["Investment Strategies", "A PIPE investment by a Category II AIF means investing in:", ["A listed company through a private placement", "An unlisted start-up's seed round", "Government bonds", "Real estate projects"], 0, "PIPE is a private investment in public equity — a negotiated placement in a listed company."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A manager discovers that an investee's CFO is the manager's partner's relative. The manager should:", ["Disclose the relationship and follow the conflict policy", "Ignore it, since a family relationship has no bearing on the fund's interests", "Hide it from the investment committee", "Immediately sell the stake at any price"], 0, "Potential conflicts involving relatives of the manager's team must be disclosed and handled under the fund's conflict-of-interest policy."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Which party in a trust-based Category II AIF usually appoints the investment manager?", ["The trustee", "The investee companies", "The registrar", "The placement agent"], 0, "The trustee, acting for the trust, appoints the manager through an investment management agreement."], ["Investments Landscape", "Why are pension funds generally considered natural long-term investors in private markets?", ["They need daily liquidity for all benefits", "They are barred by their regulators from holding any listed equities at all", "They pay no attention to risk", "Their liabilities stretch decades into the future"], 3, "Pension liabilities are long-dated and predictable, allowing part of the portfolio to be locked up for higher returns."], ["Valuation", "Revenue multiples are most often used to value:", ["Mature utilities with steady earnings", "Government bonds", "Early-stage companies with little or no profit", "Cash balances"], 2, "When earnings are negative or volatile, revenue multiples provide a usable reference."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Before launching a scheme, an AIF must file its PPM with SEBI through:", ["Its statutory auditor", "A stock exchange", "A SEBI-registered merchant banker", "The trustee alone"], 2, "PPMs must be filed through a merchant banker who conducts due diligence."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Which governance practice reduces the risk of biased investment decisions?", ["Decisions taken by one senior partner alone, without minutes or written records", "Recusal of conflicted members and recorded IC minutes", "Allowing founders to vote in the IC", "Skipping IC review for small deals"], 1, "Recusal of conflicted members and recorded minutes make investment committee decisions objective, traceable and accountable to investors."], ["Types of Investments", "Venture debt carries more risk than a typical corporate loan mainly because borrowers:", ["Have long records of stable profits and plenty of fixed assets to pledge", "Are government-owned entities", "Have no equity investors", "Are often loss-making with limited collateral"], 3, "Venture borrowers depend on future equity funding and usually lack cash flows and hard assets, so lenders rely on investor backing and warrants."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Under SEBI's stewardship code, an AIF should:", ["Avoid any engagement with investee management", "Vote only as the promoter wishes", "Keep its voting policy secret", "Monitor and engage with investee companies and disclose voting policy"], 3, "The stewardship code requires institutional investors, including AIFs, to have policies on monitoring, engagement and voting."], ["Taxation", "The annual statement issued by a Category I or II AIF to investors helps them:", ["Redeem units early", "Report each type of allocated income correctly", "Avoid KYC", "Compute the fund's IRR"], 1, "Investors need the breakdown of income by nature to file returns under pass-through."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "The annual PPM compliance audit for an AIF checks:", ["Only the investees' accounts", "Adherence to the terms disclosed in the PPM", "Investors' income tax", "The stock exchange's operations"], 1, "It verifies that the scheme has followed its PPM, with findings reported to the trustee, manager and SEBI as required."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "SEBI's performance benchmarking framework for AIFs aims mainly to:", ["Fix minimum returns for each fund", "Give investors industry-wide data for comparing funds", "Replace valuation by independent valuers with an index-based valuation", "Set management fees"], 1, "Benchmarking agencies publish industry benchmarks based on AIF data, aiding comparison and transparency."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Information covenants in a PE deal require the company to:", ["List within two years", "Provide regular financial and operational reports", "Pay a fixed dividend", "Give the fund majority control"], 1, "Information covenants give the investor periodic accounts, MIS and notices of material events for monitoring."], ["Investment Strategies", "An infrastructure fund's long-term returns depend heavily on:", ["Daily stock market movements", "Regulatory and concession terms of the projects", "The fund's office location", "Retail investor sentiment"], 1, "Infrastructure cash flows depend on tariffs, concessions and regulation, making these key risks."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Investors with an unresolved complaint against an AIF can approach:", ["The NPS Trust", "IRDAI Bima Bharosa", "SEBI SCORES and then the ODR mechanism", "The Income Tax Ombudsman"], 2, "AIF grievances go to SEBI's SCORES, with online dispute resolution available for unresolved disputes."], ["AIF Structuring", "A Category II AIF set up as an LLP gives its partners:", ["Limited liability", "Unlimited personal liability", "Exemption from SEBI rules", "Daily redemption rights"], 0, "LLPs offer limited liability to partners, though they are less common for Indian AIFs than trusts."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "Why should a 2018-vintage fund be compared with 2018-vintage peers?", ["All vintages always deliver the same returns", "SEBI requires every manager to raise exactly one fund in each calendar year", "Fees differ only by vintage", "Entry valuations and market conditions were similar"], 3, "Funds of the same vintage faced comparable entry valuations and market conditions, which makes peer comparisons fairer."], ["Investment Strategies", "The main risk in an early-stage VC strategy is:", ["Interest-rate changes on bank deposits", "A high failure rate among portfolio companies", "Low volatility of start-up valuations", "Guaranteed dilution protection"], 1, "Most early-stage companies fail or return little; returns depend on a few big winners."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Investors in a fund form an advisory committee mainly to:", ["Run the day-to-day portfolio of the fund in place of the investment manager", "Review conflicts and give certain consents", "Audit the fund's financial statements", "Act as custodian of fund assets"], 1, "LP advisory committees advise on conflicts of interest, valuation questions and consents required under the documents."], ["Investments Landscape", "Which statement about private markets versus public markets is most accurate?", ["Private markets publish transaction prices continuously, like stock exchanges", "Public markets have no regulation", "Private markets are free of all risk", "Private markets have less disclosure and price discovery"], 3, "Private markets rely on negotiated deals with limited disclosure and periodic valuations, unlike continuously priced, highly regulated public markets."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "A 'management fee offset' means that:", ["Investors pay double management fees whenever the fund makes a new investment", "The manager pays investors a fee", "Carry is reduced to zero", "Transaction or monitoring fees reduce the management fee"], 3, "Fees the manager earns from portfolio companies are partly or fully credited against management fees, so investors are not charged twice."], ["Taxation", "A Category II AIF must deduct TDS on income credited to non-resident investors at:", ["Zero in all cases", "A fixed 1%", "The resident TDS rate only, as treaty rates cannot be applied by AIFs", "Rates in force, or treaty rates where eligible"], 3, "For non-residents, TDS applies at rates in force, and treaty rates may be applied if documentation is provided."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "A term sheet is usually signed:", ["After the fund exits the investment", "Before detailed due diligence and final documents", "After closing and funding", "Only when the company lists"], 1, "The term sheet records key commercial terms and often grants exclusivity before detailed diligence."], ["Taxation", "An investor who sells AIF units before the minimum holding period may lose the ability to:", ["Receive any distributions", "Claim TDS credit", "Set off losses passed through by the fund", "File a return"], 2, "Loss pass-through is allowed only if units are held for the prescribed period."], ["Investments Landscape", "The 'denominator effect' faced by institutional investors refers to:", ["A fall in the value of private assets caused by higher management fees in a weak market", "The rise in the number of funds in the market", "Private assets exceeding target weight after public markets fall", "The reduction in management fees over time"], 2, "When listed assets fall sharply, the slower-marked private allocation becomes a larger share of the portfolio, possibly breaching targets and limiting new commitments."], ["AIF Structuring", "An offshore feeder investing into an Indian AIF would typically be subject to:", ["No regulation at all", "Rules of both its home jurisdiction and Indian law", "Only Indian mutual fund rules", "Only US securities law"], 1, "Feeders must follow their own jurisdiction's rules and Indian regulations such as FEMA and SEBI norms."], ["Types of Investments", "A mezzanine lender often receives an 'equity kicker'. This is:", ["A higher seniority than senior secured debt", "A guarantee from the government", "Warrants or conversion rights adding upside", "An exemption from interest payments"], 2, "Equity kickers such as warrants compensate mezzanine lenders for subordination with a share in equity upside."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category I AIF with investable funds of ₹180 crore can invest at most how much in one investee company under the general limit?", ["₹45 crore", "₹18 crore", "₹36 crore", "₹90 crore"], 0, "Category I and II AIFs may invest up to 25% of investable funds in a single investee: 25% × 180 = ₹45 crore."], ["Valuation", "A loan held by a private credit fund that is performing normally is typically valued with reference to:", ["The borrower's share price only", "Discounted cash flows at a market-based yield", "Its original amount regardless of credit change", "The fund's carry"], 1, "Loans are valued by discounting expected cash flows at yields reflecting current credit risk."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A quarterly investor report of a PE fund typically includes:", ["Daily stock tips", "Other investors' personal details", "Portfolio updates, valuations and cash-flow summaries", "The manager's personal tax returns"], 2, "Quarterly reports cover portfolio performance, valuations, capital calls and distributions, and key developments in investee companies."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A client with ₹1.5 crore of total savings wants to put ₹1 crore into a PE fund. A manager should be concerned mainly about:", ["Her preferred sector for investment and whether the fund matches that sector", "Whether she lives in a metro city", "The colour of the fund's brochure", "Concentration and illiquidity relative to her wealth"], 3, "Committing two-thirds of total savings to an illiquid fund is risky and likely unsuitable, regardless of the investor's interest."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "When a fund reaches the end of its extended tenure with unsold assets, investors may approve:", ["Conversion to an open-ended mutual fund", "A dissolution period or in-specie distribution as per SEBI's framework", "Indefinite extension of the fund's life by the manager without investor approval", "Forced sale to the sponsor at cost"], 1, "SEBI provides options such as a dissolution period, liquidation scheme or in-specie distribution, with investor approval."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Commercial due diligence on a target company mainly assesses:", ["Only the company's tax filings", "Market size, competition and the business model", "The fund's own fee structure and how it compares with peer funds", "Investors' KYC documents"], 1, "Commercial diligence evaluates the market, competitive position and sustainability of the business plan."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "'Conditions precedent' in a deal document are:", ["Rights that arise only after the fund has exited the investee company", "Clauses about the fund's tax status", "Investors' redemption rights", "Requirements to be met before the investment is completed"], 3, "CPs, such as approvals and diligence closures, must be satisfied before the investor funds the deal."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "A fund's management fee steps down after the investment period mainly because:", ["The manager's workload shifts from investing to managing exits", "SEBI prohibits fees after five years", "Carry is paid instead of fees", "Investors stop paying any expenses"], 0, "Fees often fall or move to invested capital after the investment period, reflecting reduced deployment work."], ["Valuation", "A stressed company's equity is valued close to zero while its secured loans retain value because:", ["Secured lenders rank ahead in recovery", "Equity always has higher seniority", "Loans are guaranteed by SEBI", "Equity holders get paid first"], 0, "In distress, recoveries flow first to secured creditors according to priority, while equity absorbs losses and may be worth little."], ["Investments Landscape", "Compared with a listed equity fund, a Category II PE fund offers investors:", ["Daily liquidity and lower fees than an index fund", "Potential illiquidity premium with long lock-in", "Guaranteed returns because holdings are unlisted", "Freedom to redeem at NAV whenever needed"], 1, "PE funds may earn a premium for illiquidity and active ownership, but capital is locked up for years and fees are higher."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A 'no-fault' removal clause in fund documents lets investors:", ["Remove the manager only after a court has found it guilty of misconduct", "Redeem units at NAV", "Remove the manager by supermajority without proving cause", "Cancel past investments"], 2, "No-fault removal gives investors a route to replace the manager by a high vote threshold even without misconduct."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "A fund with commitments of ₹600 crore charges 2% a year on commitments during a five-year investment period. Total management fees over that period are:", ["₹12 crore", "₹60 crore", "₹30 crore", "₹120 crore"], 1, "Annual fee = 2% × 600 = ₹12 crore. Over five years: 12 × 5 = ₹60 crore."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund invested ₹25 crore in a company and realised ₹90 crore on exit. The MOIC is:", ["3.6x", "2.6x", "3.0x", "0.28x"], 0, "MOIC = 90 ÷ 25 = 3.6x. 2.6x would be the net gain multiple (65 ÷ 25)."], ["Taxation", "Carried interest structured through a separate unit class is often argued to be capital gains because:", ["It arises from units held as investments", "It is always exempt", "SEBI classifies it as salary income of the investment team members", "It is paid by the government"], 0, "Managers argue carry is investment return on carry units; authorities may contest this depending on facts."], ["Regulatory Framework", "A Category I AIF has a corpus of ₹150 crore. The minimum continuing interest is:", ["₹3.75 crore", "₹5 crore", "₹7.5 crore", "₹1.5 crore"], 0, "Lower of 2.5% of corpus (₹3.75 crore) or ₹5 crore, so ₹3.75 crore."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "An investor puts in ₹100 crore and receives ₹133.1 crore exactly three years later. The IRR is:", ["11%", "33.1%", "9%", "10%"], 3, "100 × (1 + r)³ = 133.1 gives r = 10%. 11% is the simple average of 33.1%."], ["Investment Strategies", "A fund of funds investing in Category II AIFs offers access to diverse managers but:", ["Guarantees top-quartile returns", "Adds a second layer of fees", "Removes all illiquidity", "Allows daily redemptions"], 1, "FoFs charge their own fees on top of underlying fund fees, reducing net returns."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A tax adviser's main role in an AIF is to:", ["Manage the fund's investments and take decisions on new deals", "Advise on fund structure and tax compliance", "Hold the fund's securities", "Distribute units to investors"], 1, "Tax advisers help with structuring, pass-through compliance, TDS and investor tax reporting."], ["Taxation", "Interest income earned by a Category I AIF from its debt investments is taxed:", ["At the fund level only", "As capital gains", "Exempt in all cases", "In investors' hands as interest income"], 3, "Under pass-through, interest income retains its character and is taxed in investors' hands."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "A secondary sale of a portfolio stake to another PE fund is common when:", ["The company is not ready for IPO but the fund must exit", "The company has just listed", "The fund is still in the first year of its investment period", "SEBI bans IPOs"], 0, "Sales to other financial investors provide an exit where IPO or strategic sale is unavailable."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "A clawback obliges the manager to:", ["Return excess carry received, after final accounting", "Return management fees each year", "Pay investors a fixed return", "Buy all unsold assets of the fund at book value at the end of its life"], 0, "If carry received exceeds the manager's entitlement on final reckoning, the clawback requires repayment."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "ESG reporting to investors helps them:", ["Compute the fund's carry", "Avoid tax on distributions", "Redeem units early", "Track how portfolio companies manage ESG risks"], 3, "ESG reports show progress on environmental, social and governance issues in the portfolio."], ["Types of Investments", "Which covenant protects a private credit lender by restricting a borrower's further borrowing?", ["A tag-along right that lets the lender sell its shares alongside the promoter", "A dividend reinvestment plan", "A negative covenant limiting additional debt", "A right of first offer on shares"], 2, "Negative covenants restrict actions such as taking more debt or selling assets, protecting the lender's position."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "After investment, value creation in a PE portfolio company often includes:", ["Strengthening management, governance and operations", "Daily trading of its shares", "Withdrawing all board involvement", "Reducing reporting to investors"], 0, "Active ownership adds value by strengthening management, systems, strategy and governance of portfolio companies, not by trading their shares."], ["Investments Landscape", "Private capital matters for the Indian economy mainly because it:", ["Replaces the need for any bank lending to small and mid-sized businesses in India", "Guarantees jobs in every company it backs", "Is available only to government-owned companies", "Funds companies that cannot easily raise bank loans or list"], 3, "Start-ups and growth firms often lack collateral or scale for bank loans or IPOs; private equity and venture capital fill that gap."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "The 100-day plan after a buyout refers to:", ["Priority actions for value creation after acquisition", "The period before SEBI registration", "The time allowed to investors to redeem units after the fund's first close", "The fund's marketing campaign"], 0, "Buyout funds agree early priorities with management, such as operational fixes and governance changes."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which of these is an obligation of the AIF manager under SEBI's framework?", ["Ensure investments follow the PPM and regulations", "Guarantee investors a minimum return each year on their contributed capital", "List units on an exchange", "Distribute profits every month"], 0, "Managers must invest per the PPM, maintain records and comply with regulations; they cannot guarantee returns."], ["Investment Process and Governance of Funds", "Legal due diligence on a target typically reviews:", ["Market growth forecasts only", "Fund investors' tax status", "Title, licences, contracts and litigation", "The fund's carry calculation"], 2, "Legal due diligence checks ownership of shares and assets, licences and approvals, material contracts and pending litigation of the target."], ["Investment Strategies", "A founder owns 60% of a company. A new round issues shares equal to 25% of the post-money company. The founder's stake becomes:", ["35%", "48%", "60%", "45%"], 3, "The new round dilutes all existing holders by 25%, so the founder holds 60% × 0.75 = 45%. 35% wrongly subtracts 25 points."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A fund lending to mid-market companies through structured debt, without leverage, would usually register as:", ["Category I SME fund", "Category III AIF", "A non-banking finance company", "Category II AIF"], 3, "Private debt funds without leverage or special incentives fall under Category II."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A legal adviser to the fund primarily helps with:", ["Selecting the stocks the fund will buy", "Calculating the NAV every day", "Raising capital from retail investors", "Drafting fund and deal documents"], 3, "Lawyers draft the PPM, trust deed, agreements and deal documents and advise on regulatory compliance."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Why is a capital-call structure important to explain to a new AIF investor?", ["She can skip drawdowns without any consequence", "The fund takes all money upfront and returns it monthly", "Capital calls are optional for all investors", "She must keep cash ready to meet drawdowns when called"], 3, "Investors commit an amount drawn over time; failing to meet calls can trigger default penalties, so liquidity planning matters."], ["Types of Investments", "For a stressed company, a special situation fund may acquire its loans from banks mainly in order to:", ["Earn the original coupon on the loans without taking any credit or recovery risk", "Drive a restructuring or resolution and gain from recovery", "Avoid all legal processes", "List the loans on a stock exchange"], 1, "Buying stressed loans at a discount gives the fund influence over restructuring or insolvency resolution and upside from recovery."], ["Investment Strategies", "A secondaries fund in private equity primarily:", ["Invests only in seed-stage start-ups through convertible notes and SAFEs", "Lends to real estate developers", "Buys existing fund interests or portfolios from other investors", "Trades listed shares daily"], 2, "Secondaries funds buy LP interests or portfolios, often at a discount, providing liquidity to sellers and shorter J-curves."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "Net IRR is usually lower than gross IRR because net IRR:", ["Includes only unrealised investments", "Ignores cash flow timing", "Deducts fees, expenses and carry", "Adds back management fees"], 2, "Net IRR measures investors' actual returns after management fees, fund expenses and carried interest, so it is below gross IRR."], ["Types of Investments", "A start-up issues CCPS to a fund at ₹100 per share with a 1x non-participating liquidation preference. On a sale at a low price, the fund will receive:", ["The higher of its preference amount or its as-converted share", "Only its as-converted share of sale proceeds, even if this is below its investment", "Twice its investment in all circumstances", "Nothing until all equity holders are paid"], 0, "A non-participating preference gives the holder either its preference amount or what it would get on conversion, whichever is higher."], ["Types of Investments", "Real estate equity funds generally earn returns from:", ["Fixed coupons guaranteed by the developers whatever the sales of the project", "Interest on government securities", "Development profits, rental income and value appreciation", "Daily trading of property shares"], 2, "Real estate equity returns come from project margins, rents and capital gains, with risks of delay and market downturns."], ["Investment Strategies", "A start-up raises ₹50 crore from a fund for a 20% post-money stake. The pre-money valuation is:", ["₹250 crore", "₹50 crore", "₹300 crore", "₹200 crore"], 3, "Post-money = 50 ÷ 20% = ₹250 crore; pre-money = 250 − 50 = ₹200 crore."], ["Valuation", "Why does SEBI require AIF valuations to follow a disclosed methodology?", ["To guarantee high returns", "To ensure consistency and allow investors to understand values", "To eliminate all professional judgement from the valuation of unlisted holdings", "To reduce taxes"], 1, "Disclosure and consistency help investors assess NAV reliability and compare over time."], ["Fund Performance and Benchmarking", "A fund has paid-in capital of ₹120 crore, distributions of ₹108 crore and NAV of ₹60 crore. Its DPI is:", ["1.4x", "0.5x", "0.9x", "1.8x"], 2, "DPI = 108 ÷ 120 = 0.9x. TVPI is 1.4x and RVPI is 0.5x."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "In a GP-led continuation fund transaction, a key safeguard for existing investors is:", ["Mandatory rollover of all investors", "No disclosure of the transfer price to the existing investors of the fund", "Manager setting the price alone", "An independent price-discovery process such as a fairness opinion"], 3, "Because the manager is on both sides, independent pricing and informed investor choice are essential."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Category I and II AIFs must be close-ended mainly because:", ["SEBI wants them to be listed on stock exchanges and traded daily like shares", "They invest only in government bonds", "Investors demand daily NAV", "Their unlisted holdings cannot be sold quickly to meet redemptions"], 3, "Illiquid unlisted investments make open-ended redemptions impractical and unfair to remaining investors."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "In a share subscription agreement, 'representations and warranties' given by the company and promoters mainly:", ["Confirm facts on which the investor relies, backed by indemnity", "Guarantee a minimum return on the investment for the life of the fund", "Fix the exit date of the fund", "Set the fund's management fee"], 0, "Reps and warranties state facts about the business; if untrue, the investor can claim under indemnity provisions."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "Why does SEBI require at least one key investment team member to hold NISM certification?", ["To exempt the manager from SEBI inspections and periodic regulatory reporting", "To guarantee investor returns", "To allow leverage", "To ensure a minimum standard of competence"], 3, "Certification ensures the team has baseline knowledge of AIF regulations and practices."], ["Taxation", "Why is the classification of exit gains as capital gains or business income important for a Category II AIF?", ["Capital gains are always exempt in the hands of AIF investors in India", "Business income is taxed at fund level, not passed through", "Business income is never taxed", "It decides SEBI registration"], 1, "Pass-through applies to non-business income; business income is taxed in the fund, changing investors' tax outcome."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Which statement on direct plans of AIFs is correct?", ["They have a longer lock-in", "They guarantee returns", "They carry no distribution fee", "They are for retail investors only"], 2, "Direct plans are for investors approaching directly or via registered advisers and carry no distribution or placement fee."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A drag-along right is typically subject to conditions such as:", ["Unlimited use at any price by any shareholder", "Approval by SEBI for every sale", "A ban on strategic buyers", "Minimum price or timing thresholds"], 3, "Founders usually negotiate safeguards like minimum valuations or time windows before a drag can be exercised."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Special situation funds are permitted to acquire stressed loans, subject to conditions, under:", ["The mutual fund regulations applicable to credit risk and debt schemes", "The insurance regulations", "SEBI's framework for special situation funds", "The PFRDA regulations"], 2, "SEBI's special situation fund framework under Category I allows acquisition of stressed loans in line with RBI's directions."], ["Types of Investments", "An optionally convertible debenture (OCD) differs from a CCPS mainly because the OCD:", ["May be repaid as debt if not converted", "Must always convert into equity on a fixed date", "Ranks below equity in a liquidation", "Cannot carry any coupon"], 0, "OCDs give the holder a choice to convert or be repaid, so they behave like debt unless converted. CCPS must convert into equity."], ["Investments Landscape", "A family office with ₹500 crore and no near-term spending needs wants to add private equity. A reasonable first step is to:", ["Set a target allocation and commit across vintages", "Invest the full amount in one fund in one year", "Choose the single fund with the highest past IRR and commit the whole amount to it", "Wait until markets are at an all-time high"], 0, "A planned target allocation, built over several vintages and managers, manages timing and manager risk. Concentrating in one fund or chasing past IRR is risky."], ["Valuation", "A company has revenue of ₹60 crore, peers trade at 4x EV/revenue, and the company holds net cash of ₹20 crore. Its equity value is:", ["₹260 crore", "₹240 crore", "₹220 crore", "₹280 crore"], 0, "EV = 60 × 4 = ₹240 crore. Net cash adds to equity: 240 + 20 = ₹260 crore."], ["Regulatory Framework", "For Category I and II AIFs, distribution fee paid upfront is limited because SEBI wants:", ["Distributors paid only at fund exit", "Most commission paid as equal trail over the fund's life", "No distribution fees at all for any plan, whether direct or regular", "Higher upfront fees for large investors"], 1, "SEBI limits upfront payment to a portion of total distribution fee, with the rest on equal trail, aligning incentives."], ["AIF Structuring", "A parallel fund structure is used when:", ["One fund invests all its money into another fund managed by the same manager", "Separate vehicles invest side by side for different investor groups", "Two managers compete for the same deals", "A fund invests in listed shares only"], 1, "Parallel funds, often onshore and offshore, invest alongside each other in agreed proportions for investors with different needs."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A sponsor of an AIF differs from the manager in that the sponsor:", ["Makes all the investment and exit decisions for the fund's portfolio", "Holds custody of assets", "Sets up the fund and maintains continuing interest", "Prepares the audit report"], 2, "The sponsor establishes the AIF and keeps the required continuing interest; the manager runs investments."], ["Valuation", "A valuer adjusts a minority stake's value for lack of marketability because:", ["The stake cannot be readily sold", "It is listed and liquid", "Minority stakes are always worth more", "SEBI fixes the discount"], 0, "Illiquid holdings typically warrant a discount reflecting difficulty of sale; the amount is judgement-based."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "An 8% hurdle compounded annually on ₹50 crore of contributed capital for two years requires investors to receive first:", ["₹58.00 crore", "₹54.00 crore", "₹66.64 crore", "₹58.32 crore"], 3, "With annual compounding, 50 × 1.08² = ₹58.32 crore must go to investors first. ₹58 crore wrongly applies simple interest."], ["Regulatory Framework", "The tenure of a close-ended Category II AIF can be extended by up to:", ["Ten years at the manager's choice", "Two years, with two-thirds investor approval by value", "Any period without approval", "Six months only, and only with SEBI's prior written approval"], 1, "Extension of up to two years is allowed with approval of two-thirds of unit holders by value."], ["Types of Investments", "Why do foreign investors in Indian start-ups often prefer CCPS over OCDs?", ["OCDs cannot be issued by Indian companies", "CCPS carry a dividend guaranteed by the company irrespective of its profits", "CCPS can be redeemed at any time", "FEMA treats CCPS as equity, while OCDs are treated as debt"], 3, "Under FEMA, instruments that compulsorily convert are equity; optionally convertible instruments are debt, subject to ECB rules."], ["AIF Ecosystem", "A fund's administrator typically handles:", ["Choosing which companies to invest in", "Capital calls, distributions and investor accounting", "Negotiating deal valuations and investment terms with the founders of investees", "Approving SEBI registration"], 1, "Fund administrators process drawdowns and distributions, maintain investor accounts and support reporting."], ["Valuation", "A down round at a much lower price than the fund's last mark signals that the fund should:", ["Reassess and likely lower the carrying value", "Keep the earlier value unchanged until the company is finally sold or listed", "Raise the value", "Stop valuing the investment"], 0, "New arm's-length pricing is strong evidence of fair value and usually requires a markdown."], ["Valuation", "Net asset value approach is most suitable for:", ["Asset-holding companies such as real estate holdcos", "High-growth software start-ups", "Companies with no assets", "Listed blue chips"], 0, "Where value lies mainly in underlying assets, summing their fair values less liabilities is appropriate."], ["AIF Structuring", "Under SEBI's rules, co-investment by a Category I or II AIF's investors in an investee company must be:", ["Always at a lower price than the AIF paid, to reward the co-investors", "Kept undisclosed to other investors", "On terms not more favourable than the AIF's", "Made only by the manager's relatives"], 2, "SEBI requires co-investment terms to be no more favourable than those of the AIF for the same investee, among other conditions."], ["AIF Structuring", "Investors of a Category I or II AIF generally have rights that are:", ["Pro-rata to their commitments, subject to permitted exceptions", "Decided by the manager case by case for each investor, without any disclosure", "Equal irrespective of commitment", "Determined by the investee"], 0, "SEBI requires pro-rata rights in line with commitments, with limited permitted differential rights."], ["Fund Monitoring, Reporting and Exit", "Lock-in rules after an IPO affect a PE fund's exit mainly by:", ["Allowing immediate full sale on listing day", "Cancelling the shares", "Increasing carry automatically", "Delaying the sale of pre-IPO shares"], 3, "Pre-IPO shareholders may face lock-ins, so funds often exit gradually after listing."], ["Taxation", "A Category I AIF trust's business income, once taxed at fund level, is in investors' hands generally:", ["Taxed again at slab rates", "Taxed as capital gains", "Exempt on distribution", "Subject to GST"], 2, "Business income taxed at fund level is not taxed again when distributed."], ["AIF Structuring", "A manager structures a scheme for accredited investors only. A key benefit is:", ["Exemption from SEBI registration and from filing a placement memorandum", "Certain regulatory relaxations available to such schemes", "Guaranteed tax exemption", "Permission to accept retail investors"], 1, "Accredited-investor-only schemes and large value funds receive some relaxations, as such investors are considered better able to assess risk."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A fund will invest in road and renewable energy projects and seeks to register under a category viewed as economically desirable. It should register as:", ["Category III long-short fund", "Category II fund of funds", "Category I infrastructure fund", "A real estate investment trust"], 2, "Infrastructure funds are a Category I sub-category. REITs and InvITs are listed trusts under separate SEBI regulations."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Why do investors negotiate a cap on the fund's organisational expenses in the LPA or contribution agreement?", ["To increase the manager's carry", "To limit set-up costs charged to the fund", "To allow early redemption", "To avoid KYC"], 1, "Caps on organisational expenses prevent excessive legal and set-up costs from being charged to the fund and reducing investors' returns."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "Fund capital is ₹150 crore, total proceeds ₹240 crore and the preferred return is ₹12 crore. With 20% carry and no catch-up, the carry is:", ["₹18.0 crore", "₹15.6 crore", "₹2.4 crore", "₹48.0 crore"], 1, "Profit = ₹90 crore; above hurdle = 90 − 12 = ₹78 crore; carry = 20% × 78 = ₹15.6 crore."], ["Fee Structure of AIFs", "For the same fund (capital ₹150 crore, proceeds ₹240 crore, preferred return ₹12 crore, 20% carry) with a full catch-up, carry is:", ["₹15.6 crore", "₹12 crore", "₹18 crore", "₹20 crore"], 2, "With full catch-up and sufficient profits, the manager receives 20% of total profit: 20% × 90 = ₹18 crore."], ["Investments Landscape", "Which type of investor commonly acts as an anchor in Indian infrastructure and SME funds, often with development objectives?", ["Retail investors through monthly SIPs in liquid funds", "Corporate treasuries parking surplus cash for a week", "Day traders seeking intraday gains", "Development finance institutions"], 3, "Development finance institutions and government-backed funds frequently anchor funds that support infrastructure and SMEs. Short-horizon investors cannot commit to illiquid funds."], ["AIF Structuring", "Why might a manager offer a separate unit class to an anchor investor with a fee discount?", ["To hide fees from other investors", "To allow the anchor investor to take over management of the fund's portfolio", "To reward a large early commitment, as disclosed", "To avoid KYC of the anchor"], 2, "Fee discounts for anchors are common if disclosed and consistent with SEBI's framework on differential rights."], ["Taxation", "GST on the management fee of a Category II AIF effectively:", ["Reduces the manager's income tax", "Increases the cost borne by investors", "Is paid by the investee companies", "Applies only to carry"], 1, "GST is charged on fees and usually cannot be offset by the fund, raising investor costs."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "A default by an investor on a capital call may lead to:", ["The fund being dissolved automatically and its assets distributed to the others", "SEBI paying the amount", "Penalties such as interest, suspension of rights or forced sale", "Higher returns for the defaulter"], 2, "Default clauses protect the fund and other investors through penalties and possible forfeiture."], ["Legal Documentation and Negotiations", "Affirmative or veto rights in a shareholders' agreement allow a minority PE investor to:", ["Run day-to-day operations of the company", "Receive a guaranteed dividend", "Block key decisions without its consent", "Force the company to list within a year"], 2, "Veto rights over matters such as new issuances, budgets and related-party deals protect minority investors."], ["Investments Landscape", "An insurer considering a private equity allocation must mainly reconcile it with:", ["The daily NAV movements of the liquid mutual fund units it holds for claims", "Its brand advertising budget for the year", "Its liability profile and regulatory investment limits", "The number of agents it employs"], 2, "Insurers invest against long-term liabilities and are subject to IRDAI investment norms, which determine how much illiquid exposure they can take."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "Which investor is the best fit for a Category I venture capital fund?", ["A retiree who needs stable monthly income from a modest savings corpus", "A saver who wants capital protection", "A trader seeking returns within a month", "One who can tolerate high failure rates and a long lock-in"], 3, "VC investing involves many failures and a few big winners over long periods, suiting investors with high risk tolerance and patience."], ["AIFs in India and Its Suitability", "A Category II AIF plans to invest mainly in units of other AIFs. It is a:", ["Special situation fund", "Social impact fund", "Fund of funds", "Angel fund"], 2, "A fund that invests in other AIFs is a fund of funds, typically registered under Category II."]]};
